{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Problem\n\nOn arm64 macOS hosts, 17 tests in the wiki-render suite fail because the pandoc executable bundled by `pypandoc-binary==1.17` is an **x86_64** binary and cannot execute.\n\nThe cause is upstream, not ours. Every layer under our control is correct — the pin, the lock, the resolver, the interpreter, and the installed wheel tag. `pypandoc-binary`'s `macosx_11_0_arm64` wheel ships an x86_64 payload.\n\nEvidence recorded in the root-cause comment on this ticket:\n\n- The installed dist-info reports `Tag: py3-none-macosx_11_0_arm64`, installed by `uv`, under an arm64 interpreter on an arm64 host.\n- `file` on the binary inside that wheel reports `Mach-O 64-bit executable x86_64`, confirmed against a fresh download whose sha256 matches `uv.lock:2399` exactly.\n- Across every version publishing a macOS arm64 wheel — 1.15, 1.16, 1.16.2, 1.17 — the arm64 wheel is within one byte of its x86_64 sibling, consistent with a copied payload. 1.17 is the latest release.\n\n**Moving the pin is therefore not a remedy.** No published version of `pypandoc-binary` ships a working arm64 macOS pandoc.\n\n## Why skipping is forbidden\n\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_wiki_render.py:6-9` states that these tests never `importorskip` and that a silently absent or drifted pandoc must FAIL, not skip. ADR 0095 makes the `wiki` extra required. Any remedy that converts these failures into skips is rejected on arrival.\n\nThe sibling suite `test_wiki_render_hardening.py:55-56` already gates on `_NEEDS_PANDOC` and is out of scope; it skips by design.\n\n## Approach\n\n`pypandoc` resolves the executable through `_ensure_pandoc_path()`, whose search order begins:\n\n```python\nsearch_paths = [\"pandoc\", included_pandoc]\n```\n\nA pandoc on `PATH` is consulted **before** the bundled binary, and the resolver accepts only a candidate it can actually invoke. An explicit `PYPANDOC_PANDOC` environment variable overrides the whole search. So a system arm64 pandoc already wins over the unrunnable bundled one, and this is fixable **without changing any product code**.\n\nThe work is to make that system pandoc a stated, verified requirement on arm64 macOS rather than an accident of whatever a developer happens to have installed.\n\nThe one real risk is version drift. The renderer's safety tests assert byte-level output, which is exactly why ADR 0095 pins the version exactly — pandoc's `jira` writer changes its escaping between releases. A system pandoc of a different version can therefore change rendered bytes and break those assertions. The pinned wheel's bundled pandoc version must be determined and the system pandoc matched to it; if no matching build is obtainable, that finding must be recorded here and the ticket re-planned rather than worked around.\n\n`pyproject.toml` keeps `pypandoc-binary==1.17` unchanged. It remains correct on x86_64 macOS, Linux, and Windows, and is the source of truth for which pandoc version is expected everywhere.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Adding a third pandoc detector. Two already exist — `wiki_render._pandoc_path()` at `wiki_render.py:134-147` and `rebar._capabilities.is_available('datacenter_wiki')` at `_capabilities.py:245`.\n- Changing the `pypandoc-binary` pin or switching to bare `pypandoc`.\n- The 12 non-pandoc failures tracked by `985f`.\n- `test_wiki_render_hardening.py`, which gates deliberately.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The pandoc version bundled in `pypandoc-binary==1.17` is identified and recorded in a comment on this ticket. Verified by the comment naming a specific version string and the method used to extract it.\n- [ ] `docs/local-dev-env.md` documents that arm64 macOS hosts require a system pandoc at that identified version, states why the bundled binary cannot be used, and gives the install command. Verified by `grep -qi 'arm64' docs/local-dev-env.md && grep -qi 'pandoc' docs/local-dev-env.md` exiting 0, and by reading the added section.\n- [ ] With a matching system pandoc installed and on `PATH`, `python -c \"import pypandoc; print(pypandoc.get_pandoc_path())\"` prints a path that `file` reports as `arm64`. Verified by running both commands and comparing the output.\n- [ ] `pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_wiki_render.py` reports 0 failures and 0 skips on an arm64 macOS host. Verified by reading the pytest summary line.\n- [ ] The byte-level safety assertions in the wiki-render suite pass against the system pandoc, confirming no escaping drift against the pinned version. Verified by the same pytest run reporting 0 failures.\n- [ ] `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_wiki_render.py:6-9` still states the never-skip invariant, and no `skipif`, `importorskip`, or equivalent gate was added to that module. Verified by `grep -cE 'importorskip|skipif' tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_wiki_render.py` reporting 0.\n- [ ] `pyproject.toml` still pins `pypandoc-binary==1.17`. Verified by `grep -q 'pypandoc-binary==1.17' pyproject.toml` exiting 0.\n- [ ] No product code under `src/` is modified. Verified by `git diff --name-only origin/main | grep -c '^src/'` reporting 0.\n\n## Verification note\n\nThe arm64 criteria are verified on a developer arm64 macOS host, not in CI, because the project's CI runners are x86_64 and the defect is by definition invisible there. This is stated explicitly rather than left implicit: the closing evidence for those criteria is the recorded local run output, posted as a comment on this ticket. The x86_64 path stays covered by existing CI, which must continue to report the same test count as before."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1789695398885171973,"uuid":"ce68c032-f16c-4c08-b0ad-b90af2c3143d"}